📘 Jenkins Visible Execution Documentation for eMatch Katalon Pipelines
🧭 Main Overview
Purpose

This implementation was designed to run Katalon Windows desktop suites from Jenkins in a real visible desktop session, while keeping the execution stable, reusable, and easy to extend for new suites.

It solves the main limitations of running Windows UI automation directly from a Jenkins service session.

Core objectives
✅ Run tests in a visible Windows desktop session
✅ Start WinAppDriver outside the Jenkins service context
✅ Reuse the same generic scheduled task for multiple suites
✅ Keep one Jenkins pipeline file and one CMD file per suite
✅ Generate reports in the workspace used by the current build
✅ Return Jenkins result based on execution success, not test assertions
✅ Allow one pipeline to trigger the next one
✅ Ensure the current execution is fully stopped before the next one starts
🏗️ Architecture Summary

This implementation has 4 layers.

1. Jenkins pipeline file

One Groovy file per suite or suite collection.

Examples:

jenkins/ArchiveMode.groovy
jenkins/ModuleDesResponsabilite.groovy
2. Suite launcher CMD file

One CMD file per suite or suite collection.

Examples:

jenkins/ArchiveMode.cmd
jenkins/ModuleDesResponsabilite.cmd
3. Generic visible execution runner

A shared runner started through Task Scheduler.

It is responsible for:

reading the execution context
launching Katalon visibly
updating the result file
writing logs
4. Windows Scheduled Tasks

Two scheduled tasks are reused by all pipelines:

startWinDriver
\eMatch Automation\RunEMatchCommand
🔄 End to End Execution Flow
Step 1. Jenkins starts the pipeline

The pipeline checks out the repository and calls its suite specific CMD file.

Example:

bat 'call "%WORKSPACE%\\jenkins\\ArchiveMode.cmd"'
Step 2. The CMD file prepares the execution context

The CMD file writes execution data into a shared context file, such as:

D:\Projects\eMatchStarters\run-context.properties

It includes:

run id
workspace path
suite collection path
execution profile
browser type
Katalon home
Step 3. Jenkins starts the scheduled tasks

The CMD file triggers:

startWinDriver
\eMatch Automation\RunEMatchCommand

This ensures the visible UI execution happens in the interactive desktop session.

Step 4. The visible runner reads the context

The generic scheduled task reads the context file and launches Katalon with the correct suite and workspace.

Step 5. The CMD file waits for completion

The CMD file monitors the shared result file:

D:\Projects\eMatchStarters\run-result.properties

It waits until:

STATUS=FINISHED
Step 6. Jenkins prints logs and report summary

At the end, the CMD prints:

task status snapshot
result file contents
starter logs
Katalon console log
reports summary
Step 7. Jenkins stops the current execution cleanly

The pipeline explicitly stops:

scheduled tasks
WinAppDriver
Katalon processes

Only after that, it may trigger the next pipeline.

📄 Page 2. Scheduled Tasks
🖥️ Task 1. startWinDriver
Responsibility

This task starts WinAppDriver in the interactive desktop session.

Why it exists

Jenkins running as a service cannot reliably provide the visible desktop session needed by Windows UI automation.
WinAppDriver must be started where the UI actually exists.

Expected behavior
start before Katalon
remain available during execution
be stopped by the pipeline after the suite finishes
🖥️ Task 2. \eMatch Automation\RunEMatchCommand
Responsibility

This is the generic visible runner.

It reads the context file and runs the requested suite or collection without needing one dedicated scheduled task per suite.

Why it exists

Without this generic task, every new suite would require:

a new scheduled task
a new custom launcher
more maintenance

With this model, new suites only need:

one Jenkins Groovy file
one CMD file
📄 Page 3. Jenkins Pipeline File Pattern
📌 Example responsibility

A Jenkins Groovy file is responsible for:

selecting the node
checking out the repository
calling the suite CMD file
forcing cleanup at the end
optionally triggering the next Jenkins pipeline
returning SUCCESS when execution completed, even if some tests failed
✅ Standard pipeline behavior
It should:
use disableConcurrentBuilds()
run on the correct Windows node
call only its own CMD file
stop all related processes before leaving
trigger the next pipeline only after cleanup
It should not:
leave WinAppDriver alive
leave Katalon alive
keep the build hanging while the next job runs
mark Jenkins as failed just because a test case failed
